EXCEPTION HANDLING — QUICK REVISION

1. Exception disrupts normal program execution.

2. try → Code that may cause exception.

3. catch → Handles exception.

4. finally → Cleanup code; generally executes regardless
   of whether an exception occurs.

5. throw → Explicitly throws an exception.

6. throws → Declares exception in method signature.

7. Checked Exception
   → Compiler checks it.
   → Example: IOException

8. Unchecked Exception
   → RuntimeException and its subclasses.
   → Example: NullPointerException

9. Error and Exception are both under Throwable.

10. Multiple catch blocks can be used.

11. A try block must be followed by catch or finally.

12. One try can have multiple catch blocks.

13. Parent exception catch should come AFTER child exception catch.

14. Exception handling helps prevent abnormal termination
    and allows the program to handle failures gracefully.